# ============================================================================
#  My changes to the EnergyScope-Quebec data
# ============================================================================
#  How it works
#    1. Remove the "#" at the start of a line to turn it on (a line that starts with "#" is ignored).
#    2. Change the number, save this file.
#    3. In the notebook, run the cell that uses this file.
#
#  Everything you write here is applied on top of the standard data. The standard
#  data files are never modified, so you can always come back by putting the "#" back.
#
#  Reading a line:   let  <parameter>[ <year>, <technology or resource> ]  :=  <new value> ;
#     * "y" stands for the year of the calculation, so these lines work for any year.
#     * To change a value relative to its current one, use the parameter itself:
#          c_inv[y,'WIND_ONSHORE'] * 0.7      means  "30 % cheaper than today"
#     * Every line ends with a semicolon ( ; )
#     * Names must exist: a misspelled parameter or technology stops the calculation with an error that gives the line
#       and suggests the closest name.
#
#  Useful parameters
#     c_inv    investment cost of a technology           [M$CAD per GW]
#     c_maint  operation and maintenance cost            [M$CAD per GW per year]
#     f_max    maximum installable capacity              [GW]
#     f_min    minimum installable capacity              [GW]
#     lifetime lifetime of a technology                  [years]
#     c_op     cost of a resource, for each month        [M$CAD per GWh]
#     avail    available quantity of a resource          [GWh per year]
# ============================================================================


# --- Example 1: natural gas becomes 50 % more expensive (all 12 months) ------
# (switched on in the delivered file: put a # back at the start of the line to switch it off)
for {y in YEARS, m in PERIODS} { let c_op[y,'NG_EHP',m] := c_op[y,'NG_EHP',m] * 1.5; }


